v><div class=3D"container-fluid p-0"><header id=3D"about" class=3D"exemptio=
ns-section p-3 p-lg-5 d-flex d-column"><div class=3D"my-auto"><h1 class=3D"=
mb-0"> Bankruptcy Exemptions<br><span class=3D"state-name">Colorado</span><=
/h1><div class=3D"subheading mb-5"> an informational resource provided by <=
a href=3D"https://ohlawacolorado.com/" target=3D"_blank">The Law Offices of=
 Owen Hathaway</a> =E2=80=A2 <a href=3D"tel:+1-970-818-3052">970-818-3052</=
a></div><div class=3D"subheading mb-5 font-italic"> Information current as =
of November 2022</div><p class=3D"mb-5"> Bankruptcy can be scary. You're al=
ready not answering the phone because you know it's just another creditor a=
sking for money you don't have. Now you've decided to take care this proble=
m by filing for bankruptcy protection, which could be a fantastic turning p=
oint in your life, but now you're worried that some of the things you need =
to survive might get taken in the bankruptcy. Fortunately, the bankruptcy c=
ode provides a certain amount of relief from this particular worry. It's ca=
lled Exemptions. </p><p class=3D"mb-5"> Yes, once you file a Chapter 7 bank=
ruptcy petition, everything you own is subject to being taken and sold to p=
ay off some of your debt. However, you get to protect a whole bunch of thin=
gs using Exemptions. Exemptions essentially take the covered property back =
out of the bankruptcy estate (If you don't know about the bankruptcy estate=
, you should ask your attorney. It's important to understand.) </p><p class=
=3D"mb-5"> This site provides a description of the exemptions provided for =
debtors in the State of Colorado. Folks who live in Colorado and file for b=
ankruptcy protection may use these rules to protect their assets from being=
 seized by the trustee. Every state has its own rules, so don't assume any =
of these particular rules apply anywhere but Colorado. There are also compl=
icated timing rules to figure out if you are allowed to use Colorado's exem=
ptions even if you've lived here for a couple years. You may have to use an=
other state's exemptions or even the federal exemptions depending on your s=
ituation. </p><p class=3D"mb-5"> An attorney licensed to practice in Colora=
do state and federal courts created this site. The information here is not =
legal advice though. These things can get real complicated. If you have any=
 questions, you should ask an attorney to help you figure out how these rul=
es work. </p><p class=3D"mb-5"> One interesting thing: these rules can also=
 be used in non-bankruptcy debt collection scenarios as well. In fact, the =
state code does not mention bankruptcy much at all. The federal bankruptcy =
code just uses a neat constitutional waiver of federal law preemption here =
to permit states to make their own rules. (sorry, nerd lawyer moment there)=
 </p></div></header><section id=3D"homestead" class=3D"exemptions-section p=
-3 p-lg-5 d-flex flex-column mb-auto"><h2 class=3D"mb-5">Homestead</h2><!--=
--><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-=
md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Homestead - Standard</h3><d=
iv class=3D"subheading mb-3">$250,000</div><div>Colorado provides a standar=
d homestead exemption of $250,000 provided the property is actually occupie=
d by the debtor/owner or her family. This amount may not be doubled for spo=
uses.</div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div=
 class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 =C2=A7 38-41-201, 38-41-202</div><div=
 class=3D"text-primary">In re Howe, Bankr.Colo. No. 81 B 03865 C</div></div=
></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content =
col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Homestead - Mobile Home=
s</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">$250,000 or $350,000</div><div>Colorad=
o recognizes mobile homes, manufactured homes, trailers, and trailer coache=
s as "homes" for the purpose of the homestead exemption with all the rights=
 and limitations of a traditional home. Mobile homes and manufactured homes=
 must have been purchased after January 1, 1983 to qualify; trailers and tr=
ailer coaches must have been purchased after July 1, 2000.</div></div><div =
class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary=
">C.R.S. =C2=A7 38-41-201.6</div></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item =
row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-u=
ppercase mb-0">Homestead - Disabled or Elderly</h3><div class=3D"subheading=
 mb-3">$350,000</div><div><p>Colorado extends the homestead exemption to $3=
50,000 for the following debtors:</p> <ul>
  <li>elderly owners</li>
  <li>disabled owners</li>
  <li>an owner who lives with an elderly spouse</li>
  <li>an owner who lives with a disabled spouse</li>
  <li>an owner who lives with an elderly dependent</li>
  <li>an owner who lives with a disabled dependent</li>
</ul> <p class=3D"fst-italic">for this exemption, "elderly" means 60 years =
or older</p> <p class=3D"fst-italic">for this exemption, "disabled" is a mu=
lti-factor test, which means you'll have to prove it and perhaps make an ar=
gument.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right=
"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 38-41-201(1)(b)</div></div></di=
v><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-m=
d-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Homestead - Surviving Spouse=
 or children</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">same as the deceased owner<=
/div><div>Colorado permits the surviving spouse or minor children to assert=
 a deceased owner's homestead exemption.</div></div><div class=3D"exemption=
s-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 38=
-41-204</div></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"e=
xemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Buria=
l Plot</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited</div><div><p>Colorado p=
rovides full exemption for burial plots for the debtor and each dependent.<=
/p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div c=
lass=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(d)</div></div></div></sect=
ion><section id=3D"wages" class=3D"exemptions-section p-3 p-lg-5 d-flex fle=
x-column mb-auto"><h2 class=3D"mb-5">Wages</h2><!----><div class=3D"exempti=
ons-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=
=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Wages</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">80% exemp=
t - 20% subject to levy</div><div><p>Colorado exempts 80% of wages. This me=
ans that the bankruptcy trustee can take up to 20% of pre-petition wages, e=
ven if you were paid the wages after you file. This makes the date of your =
filing very important to figuring out how this exemption applies.</p> <p>If=
 your exempt wages are less than 40 hours of the current minimum wage, you =
can exempt up to that amount regardless of the 20% rule. So for an easy exa=
mple, if you make $12.32 per hour (current Colorado minimum wage) and work =
40 hours a week, your entire paycheck could be exempt. This gets complicate=
d based on filing dates, lookback periods, and other things, so make sure t=
o get an attorney's help with this.</p> <p>Oh yeah, "wages" means money you=
 earn for working. If your income comes from other sources, there may be ot=
her (and better) exemptions that apply. Keep reading =E2=80=A6</p></div></d=
iv><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text=
-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 13-54-104</div></div></div></section><section id=3D=
"insurance" class=3D"exemptions-section p-3 p-lg-5 d-flex flex-column mb-au=
to"><h2 class=3D"mb-5">Insurance</h2><div><p>Ok, insurance is complicated a=
ll by itself. Try to figure out how it works in bankruptcy? Oh boy! Here ar=
e the rules in Colorado. If you don't get it, it may not apply to you. Defi=
nitely ask for help if you think it might.</p></div><div class=3D"exemption=
s-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D=
"text-uppercase mb-0">Life Insurance - Group Policies</h3><div class=3D"sub=
heading mb-3">Unlimited</div><div><p>Colorado exempts 100% of the debtor's =
ownership interest in a group life insurance policy. These are the types of=
 policies folks get as a benefit from their employer. They usually aren't w=
orth anything unless the person dies, so this exemption is rarely used. It =
does cover the proceeds of the policy if it does pay a death benefit though=
, so beneficiaries don't have to use the insurance money to pay off any deb=
t.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><di=
v class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 10-7-205</div></div></div><div class=
=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"=
><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Life Insurance - Contractual Restriction=
s</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited</div><div><p>Colorado allows=
 a full exemption for an insurance contract, including those for annuities,=
 to restrict the rights of anyone other than the policy owner to take the v=
alue or benefits of the policy. This is highly specific to the language in =
the contract and may or may not apply depending on the how the specific pol=
icy contract is written.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-m=
d-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 10-7-106</div>=
</div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-con=
tent col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Life Insurance - C=
ash Surrender Value</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">$250,000 if owned fo=
r 4 years</div><div><p>Colorado allows an exemption of $250,000 for the cas=
h surrender value of a life insurance policy, but only if the policy has be=
en owned for more than 4 years. This exemption does not cover the amount of=
 the cash value that is a result of contributions to the insurance policy o=
ver and above the premium that have been made in the past 4 years.</p> <p>I=
n other words, if you've paid only the required premiums, then you're cover=
ed if you've owned the policy for 4 years. If you've paid extra into the po=
licy, then you'll need to do some math to figure out how much this exemptio=
n will cover.</p> <p>This one doesn't make sense to a lot of people, and th=
at's okay. Some people use life insurance as an investment rather than as a=
 way to provide death benefits. If you aren't investing in life insurance, =
this doesn't apply to you. If you own a whole life, universal life, or othe=
r similar policy, figuring this exemption will take some work.</p></div></d=
iv><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text=
-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(l)(I)(A)</div></div></div><div class=
=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"=
><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Insurance Proceeds from Loss</h3><div cl=
ass=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited =E2=80=A6 sort of</div><div><p>Colorado a=
llows an exemption of the value of insurance payments for loss, destruction=
 or damage to personal property that is also exempt.</p> <p>This exemption =
is limited to the exemption that the property would have had if it hadn't b=
een lost.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-rig=
ht"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(m)</div></div></=
div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col=
-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Insurance Proceeds from Lo=
ss of Homestead</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited =E2=80=A6 sort=
 of</div><div><p>Colorado allows an exemption of the value of insurance pay=
ments for loss, destruction or damage to real property that is also exempt.=
</p> <p>This exemption is limited to the exemption that the property would =
have had if it were sold.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-=
md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 38-41-209</di=
v></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-c=
ontent col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Accident or Sick=
ness (Disability) Insurance Proceeds</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">$20=
0 per month or Unlimited</div><div><p>Colorado allows an exemption of $200 =
of monthly payments for disability insurance benefits.</p> <p>If you sue to=
 recover for the accident and get a settlement or win, then the proceeds of=
 the suit are 100% exempt.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col=
-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 10-16-212</d=
iv></div></div></section><section id=3D"auto" class=3D"exemptions-section p=
-3 p-lg-5 d-flex flex-column mb-auto"><h2 class=3D"mb-5">Automobile</h2><!-=
---><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col=
-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Motor Vehicles</h3><div cl=
ass=3D"subheading mb-3">$15,000</div><div><p>Colorado allows an exemption o=
f $15,000 to apply towards up to two motor vehicles.</p> <p>Joint debtors a=
re allowed to double this exemption.</p> <p>This exemption does not apply t=
o snowmobiles, ATVs, golf carts, boats or other watercraft, travel trailers=
, tent trailers, or motor homes. Although the homestead exemption might app=
ly if you're living in the trailer or motor home.</p></div></div><div class=
=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R=
.S. =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(j)(I)</div></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-ite=
m row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text=
-uppercase mb-0">Motor Vehicles - Elderly or Disabled</h3><div class=3D"sub=
heading mb-3">$25,000</div><div><p>Colorado allows an exemption of $25,000 =
to apply towards up to two motor vehicles if the debtor is elderly or disab=
led. This exemption also applies if the debtor has an elderly or disabled s=
pouse or dependent.</p> <p>Joint debtors are allowed to double this exempti=
on.</p> <p>This exemption does not apply to snowmobiles, ATVs, golf carts, =
boats or other watercraft, travel trailers, tent trailers, or motor homes. =
Although the homestead exemption might apply if you're living in the traile=
r or motor home.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text=
-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(j)(II)</d=
iv></div></div></section><section id=3D"property" class=3D"exemptions-secti=
on p-3 p-lg-5 d-flex flex-column mb-auto"><h2 class=3D"mb-5">Personal Prope=
rty</h2><!----><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-=
content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Money in a Depo=
sitory Account</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">$2,500</div><div><p>In 20=
22, Colorado added an exemption for cash held in a depository account (i.e.=
 a bank).</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-rig=
ht"></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions=
-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Clothing</h3><=
div class=3D"subheading mb-3">$2,000</div><div><p>Colorado provides an exem=
ption of $2,000 for "necessary" clothes owned by the debtor and each depend=
ent.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><=
div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(a)</div></div></div><=
div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8=
 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Jewelry</h3><div class=3D"subhe=
ading mb-3">$2,500</div><div><p>Colorado provides an exemption of $2,500 fo=
r watches, jewelry and "articles of adornment."</p></div></div><div class=
=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R=
.S. =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(b)</div></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item r=
ow"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-up=
percase mb-0">Household Goods and Furnishings</h3><div class=3D"subheading =
mb-3">$6,000</div><div><p>Colorado provides an exemption of $6,000 for hous=
ehold goods. This exemption will cover things like your furniture, kitchen =
equipment, and the like.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-m=
d-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(e=
)</div></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exempti=
ons-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Books, Anti=
ques, Art, Collectibles</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">$2,000</div><div=
><p>Colorado provides an exemption of $2,000 for the debtor's books, family=
 pictures, and school books</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date co=
l-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 13-54-102(1=
)(c)</div></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exem=
ptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Firearms=
 and Hunting or Fishing Equipment</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">$1,000=
</div><div><p>Colorado added an exemption for firearms in 2022. This is dif=
ferent than the military equipment exemption in that it applies to everyone=
 who owns a firearm for personal safety.</p> <p>This exemption also covers =
hunting and fishing equipment too.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-=
date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 13-5=
4-102(1)(x)</div></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=
=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">=
Food and Fuel</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">$600</div><div><p>Colorado=
 provides an exemption of $600 for the debtor's food and fuel on hand. Fuel=
 could be gas in the car, but could also be propane, firewood, heating oil,=
 etc.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right">=
<div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(f)</div></div></div>=
<div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-md-=
8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Health Aids</h3><div class=3D"=
subheading mb-3">unlimited</div><div><p>Colorado provides a full exemption =
for all professionally prescribed health aids owned by the debtor or a depe=
ndent.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"=
><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(p)</div></div></div=
><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-md=
-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Security Deposits</h3><div cl=
ass=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited</div><div><p>Colorado provides a full exe=
mption for security deposits. Debtors may apply this exemption to a securit=
y deposit on a rented apartment or home as well as security deposits requir=
ed by utility companies</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md=
-4 text-md-right"></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=
=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">=
Property judgement against debtor for failure to pay tax on pension benefit=
s</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited</div><div><p>Colorado provid=
es an exemption for the benefit of states that have secured a judgement aga=
inst the debtor related to unpaid income tax on retirement benefits. This e=
nds up looking a little like a priority for state income taxes owed over ot=
her creditors' claims. In other words, it doesn't help you, it helps the st=
ate.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><=
div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(t)</div></div></div><=
/section><section id=3D"benefits" class=3D"exemptions-section p-3 p-lg-5 d-=
flex flex-column mb-auto"><h2 class=3D"mb-5">Public Benefits</h2><!----><di=
v class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 m=
r-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Veteran's Benefits</h3><div class=
=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited</div><div><p>Colorado provides a full exempt=
ion for pension, compensation, and allowances received by veterans. Widows =
and children of deceased veterans may also use this exemption.</p> <p>There=
 a few exceptions to this exemption for things like fraud, restitution, and=
 child support.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-=
md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(h)</div></=
div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-conte=
nt col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Earned Income Tax Cr=
edit (EIC or EITC) and Additional Child Credit</h3><div class=3D"subheading=
 mb-3">Unlimited</div><div><p>Colorado provides a full exemption for the fe=
deral Earned Income Tax Credit and the federal Additional Child Tax Credit.=
</p> <p>There a few exceptions to this exemption for things like fraud, res=
titution, and child support.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date c=
ol-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 13-54-102(=
1)(o)</div></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exe=
mptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Disabil=
ity Benefits</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">$5,000 per month</div><div>=
<p>Colorado provides a full exemption for disability benefits (not insuranc=
e) up to a limit of $4,000 per month. Disability payments above $5,000 are =
handled just like <a href=3D"https://coloradobankruptcyexemptions.com/#wage=
s">wages</a>.</p> <p>There a few exceptions to this exemption for things li=
ke fraud, restitution, and child support.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exem=
ptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=
=A7 13-54-102(1)(v)</div></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><di=
v class=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase=
 mb-0">Workers' Compensation</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited</=
div><div><p>Ok, just like the rest of the workers' compensation program, th=
is one is complicated. If you're getting or trying to get workers' comp., t=
hen you should definitely talk with an attorney to see how this one works. =
Most of these exemptions have a paragraph or two in the Colorado code. This=
 one has 8 long paragraphs.</p> <p>The way to summarize this: you get full =
exemption, if you and your employer qualify.</p></div></div><div class=3D"e=
xemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =
=C2=A7 8-42-124</div></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div cl=
ass=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-=
0">Public Assistance</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited</div><div=
><p>Colorado provides a public assistance program for folks whose income fa=
lls under certain levels or have a qualifying disability. Payments of benef=
its under this program are fully exempt.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemp=
tions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=
=A7 26-2-131</div></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=
=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">=
Unemployment Benefits</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited</div><di=
v><p>Colorado provides a full exemption for benefits received under the sta=
te's unemployment program</p> <p>The child support exception applies to thi=
s exemption.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-=
right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 8-80-103</div></div></div>=
<div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-md-=
8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Benefits from Crime Victims' C=
ompensation Fund</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited</div><div><p>=
Colorado provides a program to reimburse victims of certain crimes for thei=
r loss. The benefits paid out of this program are fully exempt.</p></div></=
div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"tex=
t-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 24-4.1-114</div><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S.=
 =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(q)</div><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S. =C2=A7 13-5=
4-102(1)(q)</div></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=
=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">=
Personal Injury Settlement or Reimbursement</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb=
-3">Unlimited =E2=80=A6 sort of</div><div><p>Colorado provides a full exemp=
tion for personal injury settlements and damages awards.</p> <p>This exempt=
ion is reduced by any amounts that the debtor owes for treatment for the in=
jury or for collection of the reimbursement (i.e. attorney's fees). Because=
 this exception to the exemption (see what I did there) can be tricky to ca=
lculate, you should get help from an attorney if this one applies to you.</=
p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div cl=
ass=3D"text-primary">C.R.S =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(n)</div></div></div><div cla=
ss=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-aut=
o"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Fraternal Society Benefits</h3><div cl=
ass=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited</div><div><p>Colorado provides a full exe=
mption for benefits a debtor receives as a member of a fraternal society. T=
he I.R.S has specific rules for how an organization must organize itself in=
 order to qualify as a fraternal society. This exemption is rarely used the=
se days, but it remains on the books.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptio=
ns-date col-md-4 text-md-right"></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item r=
ow"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-up=
percase mb-0">Stimulus Payments</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimite=
d</div><div><p>While many of the laws that provide for economic stimulus ca=
sh payments specifically exempt those payments from levy or garnishment, Co=
lorado went the extra mile and added a global exemption to the state's stat=
utes.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right">=
<div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(y)</div></div></div><=
/section><section id=3D"tools-of-trade" class=3D"exemptions-section p-3 p-l=
g-5 d-flex flex-column mb-auto"><h2 class=3D"mb-5">Tools of Trade</h2><!---=
-><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-m=
d-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Tools of Trade - Primary Occ=
upation</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">$60,000</div><div><p>Colorado pr=
ovides an exemption of up to $30,000 for tools of trade, stock in trade, fi=
xtures, books, equipment, electronics, etc. actually used in the debtor's p=
rimary occupation. This exemption may not be used on the same vehicle as th=
e <a href=3D"https://coloradobankruptcyexemptions.com/#auto">motor vehicle =
exemption</a>. So, for instance, you work in construction and own a truck w=
orth $19,500 free and clear (no loan on it). You can use this exemption on =
the truck, but all $12,000 counts against this exemption. You can't use the=
 $15,000 motor vehicle exemption on the truck and then only count $4,500 ag=
ainst the Tools of Trade exemption.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions=
-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S =C2=A7 13-5=
4-102(1)(i)</div></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=
=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">=
Tools of Trade - Secondary Occupation</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">$2=
0,000</div><div><p>Colorado provides an exemption of up to $10,000 for tool=
s of trade, stock in trade, fixtures, books, equipment, electronics, etc. a=
ctually used in the debtor's secondary occupation. This exemption may not b=
e used on the same vehicle as the <a href=3D"https://coloradobankruptcyexem=
ptions.com/#auto">motor vehicle exemption</a>. So, for instance, you work i=
n construction and own a truck worth $12,000 free and clear (no loan on it)=
. You can use this exemption on the truck, but all $12,000 counts against t=
his exemption using it up and leaving $2,000 non-exempt. Unfortunately, you=
 can't use the $7,500 motor vehicle exemption on the truck and to cover the=
 $2,000 shortfall.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 te=
xt-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(i)</div>=
</div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-con=
tent col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Military Equipment=
</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited</div><div><p>Colorado provide=
s a full exemption for military equipment personally owned by members of th=
e National Guard. This is one of the few ways to apply an exemption to fire=
arms in Colorado.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 tex=
t-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(h.5)</div=
></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-co=
ntent col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Professional's Li=
brary</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">$3,000</div><div><p>Colorado provi=
des an exemption of $3,000 for the contents of a professional's library if =
the tool of trade exemption is not applied to the same property. This exemp=
tion would apply to things like an attorney's set of reporters or a medical=
 doctor's reference books.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col=
-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(=
k)</div></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exempt=
ions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Debtor's I=
nterest in Partnership Property</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimite=
d</div><div><p>Partnerships are strange animals in the law. A person who is=
 a true general partner in a true partnership assumes a lot of personal lia=
bility. Colorado law provides some protection against personal liability ru=
nning from the partners to the partnership in the form of a full exemption =
for a partner's personal interest in property owned by a partnership. If th=
is doesn't make sense to you, don't be surprised. It's complicated. If you =
think it might apply, ask an attorney to help you figure it out.</p></div><=
/div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"te=
xt-primary">McCrimmon v. Linton, 4 Colo. App. 420, 36 P. 300 (1894)</div></=
div></div></section><section id=3D"farm" class=3D"exemptions-section p-3 p-=
lg-5 d-flex flex-column mb-auto"><h2 class=3D"mb-5">Farm Property</h2><!---=
-><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-m=
d-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Animals and Livestock, Crops=
, Farm Equipment</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">$50,000</div><div><p>Co=
lorado provides an exemption of $50,000 for livestock, crops, seed and farm=
 equipment owned by a debtor whose primary occupation is agriculture.</p> <=
p>Joint debtors only get a total of $50,000 here, so doubling this exemptio=
n is not permitted just like it is not permitted for the homestead exemptio=
n.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><di=
v class=3D"text-primary">C.R.S =C2=A7 13-54-102(1)(g)</div></div></div></se=
ction><section id=3D"federal" class=3D"exemptions-section p-3 p-lg-5 d-flex=
 flex-column mb-auto"><h2 class=3D"mb-5">Federal Non-Bankruptcy Exemptions<=
/h2><div><p>Federal law also provides a number of exemptions that a debtor =
may use in a bankruptcy case, but these exemptions aren't found in the bank=
ruptcy code. Similar to the state exemptions, these exemptions may be used =
in non-bankruptcy collection cases as well. We list some of the most common=
 here.</p></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions=
-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Military Servi=
ce Survivor's Benefits</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited</div><d=
iv><p>Federal law exempts from levy or assignment pensions and annuities pa=
id by the federal government to the survivors or deceased members of the mi=
litary. This code section contains a few carve-outs to limit double-dipping=
.</p></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div=
 class=3D"text-primary"><a class=3D"text-primary" href=3D"https://www.law.c=
ornell.edu/uscode/text/10/1450" target=3D"_blank">10 U.S.C. 1450(i)</a></di=
v></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-c=
ontent col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Civil Service Em=
ployee's Retirement Benefits</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited</=
div><div><p>Federal law exempts from levy or assignment pensions and annuit=
ies paid by the federal government to retired civil service employees.</p><=
/div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=
=3D"text-primary"><a class=3D"text-primary" href=3D"https://www.law.cornell=
.edu/uscode/text/5/8346" target=3D"_blank">5 U.S.C. 8346(a)</a></div></div>=
</div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content c=
ol-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Social Security Benefits=
</h3><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited</div><div><p>Federal law exem=
pts from levy or assignment benefits paid under the Social Security Act.</p=
></div></div><div class=3D"exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div cla=
ss=3D"text-primary"><a class=3D"text-primary" href=3D"https://www.law.corne=
ll.edu/uscode/text/42/407" target=3D"_blank">42 U.S.C. 407(a)</a></div></di=
v></div><div class=3D"exemptions-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content=
 col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D"text-uppercase mb-0">Veterans' Benefits</h3=
><div class=3D"subheading mb-3">Unlimited</div><div><p>Federal law exempts =
from levy or assignment all veterans' benefits</p> <p>The IRS gets to avoid=
 this exemption for collection of taxes</p></div></div><div class=3D"exempt=
ions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary"><a class=3D"t=
ext-primary" href=3D"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/38/5301" targe=
t=3D"_blank">38 U.S.C. 5301(a)</a></div></div></div><div class=3D"exemption=
s-item row"><div class=3D"exemptions-content col-md-8 mr-auto"><h3 class=3D=
"text-uppercase mb-0">Railroad Employee Pensions</h3><div class=3D"subheadi=
ng mb-3">Unlimited</div><div><p>Federal law exempts from levy or assignment=
 pensions paid or owed to railroad employees.</p></div></div><div class=3D"=
exemptions-date col-md-4 text-md-right"><div class=3D"text-primary"><a clas=
s=3D"text-primary" href=3D"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/45/352" =
target=3D"_blank">45 U.S.C. 352(e)</a></div></div></div></section><section =
